se_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "6bf283bc2cfddc5d9a25b353c7ac44319704195febc4ff79f0c64c7bb355b8ba" "basis" 1427 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7" "b