has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $F,$ then $F$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $F=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $F,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nF={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-17 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 21\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-25 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 27\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -20 \\\\ 20 & 28\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $S$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & 1 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -13 \\\\ 9 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 4 \\\\ 15 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A$, then  $A$ must be diagonalizable. \n\nSo, we can write $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $A$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 15 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-37603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 15 \\\\ 4 & 10\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 12 \\\\ 16 & 24\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 15 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-37271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 11\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 11\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${A}^{97},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{97} & =P{D}^{97}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{97}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{97} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{97}\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-37597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}97 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -97\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${A}^{69}$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{69} & =P{D}^{69}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{69}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{69} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{69}\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-37625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${G}^{10},$ where\n\n$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $G:$\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $G=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${G}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{G}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{10} & =P{D}^{10}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{2}^{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-2)^{10}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1024 & 0 \\\\ 1024 & 1024\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ -1024 & 1024\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ -2048 & 1024\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1024\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-140132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${C}^{36},$ where\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[18]{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[36]{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-140132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $C:$\n\n$$\nC={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[18]{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[36]{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $C=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${C}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{C}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{36} & =P{D}^{36}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[18]{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[36]{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{36}(\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt[18]{3})^{36} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[36]{3})^{36}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}27 & -3 \\\\ -27 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & 6 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-140132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37