the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{16} & =P{D}^{16}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{16}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt[8]{2})^{16} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[4]{2})^{16}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 48 \\\\ 4 & 32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-37601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-32 & -12 \\\\ 12 & -13\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 48 \\\\ 12 & 32\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}24 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 12\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $L$ given that \n\n$$\nL[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $L:$\n\n$$\nL={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $L$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $L$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-37363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]T=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]E=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-40138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\nB[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-40138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-40138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-40138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-40138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-40138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\nB[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$\n\n$$\nB={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dab0295968d3b7eb9046cbafa7795413f6df08238d363c37c8844e7b012abaae"} {"question" "q-37270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "q-37270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/state.json" "file_sha256" "24c6a70c11d05c09df365dfe349b76a146a1ea98b75744af9c49be9cddaa1a4d"} {"question" "e-7133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/f478b4206f3a8bcc5c025fa0c58bb75430bcb1493c1d8470366864f7fb7ceb6e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/example-7133.json" "file_sha256" "2c85f7f7bf720b1b09b55a3ade9658e04771ca686494e54a535e7c4a7db7a37b"} {"question" "e-7133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathcal{B}= \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PDP^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/1cf1ba5d93618341227836a110479ebfc258894f9d4f60ae4df1be682d7c74fc.png)\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/f1defa7b451fcd56632030e9c3663ad99646c5eabc98eb4e1c5b687687b9fdc4.png) Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/example-7133.json" "file_sha256" "2c85f7f7bf720b1b09b55a3ade9658e04771ca686494e54a535e7c4a7db7a37b"} {"question" "e-5502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $A^{100},$ where \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/66521b0220a4a850308d42aeccf17ce4769e563eeec6a7e1870adbcb0f46a318.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/example-5502.json" "file_sha256" "e3a4041f87218b15aaa7e61dcdde71e91c35d604dce77a18deca677a0d531549"} {"question" "e-5502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First,  notice that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/9c5de663d2cf109c4faaf839760b0afac6dcbf5ab533570100167727853cb74f.png)\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/3f73ff47f00cf345f0ac0cffd41dc325faf00c9f1d1c0ffe4ad7228e83fef3fa.png)\n\nSince $A=PDP^{-1},$ we can compute $A^k$ by using the formula \n\n$$\nA^{k} = PD^{k}P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{100} & =P{D}^{100}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{100}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{100} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {0}^{100}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/example-5502.json" "file_sha256" "e3a4041f87218b15aaa7e61dcdde71e91c35d604dce77a18deca677a0d531549"} {"question" "e-5503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $E,$ given that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/3389ba82baf25977d7b8deb9609435dce5a543a863f7d8f98102b45d770b465b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/example-5503.json" "file_sha256" "695a4b41f2e3ec9b65c509295b081be6c5a9e82011d6f8c35ab47e95eb57b2ea"} {"question" "e-5503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/9dee992fa1cc903eb23299b52080a7ebba31ef550bb22c4938c45939e8541be4.png)\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/assets/23dd5ab30b31bf85d84f290fdf9c71529cfd753b98688149f103980894ad91c6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069457/example-5503.json" "file_sha256" "695a4b41f2e3ec9b65c509295b081be6c5a9e82011d6f8c35ab47e95eb57b2ea"}] "usage" {"q-81210" [] "e-7133" [] "q-86694" [] "q-140132" [] "e-5502" [] "q-86691" [] "q-37363" [] "e-5503" [] "q-37270" [] "q-37314" [] "q-81215" [] "q-37601" [] "q-37597" [] "q-37358" [] "q-37625" [] "q-37271" []} "basis" 2372 "retractions" [[17592186124963 :question/problem "Find a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 10$ and $λ_{2} = 5$, and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186125001 :question/problem "Find $A^{97}$, where\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}$."] [17592186124299 :question/problem "Find a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186124299 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}= \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PDP^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124257 :question/problem "Find $A^{100},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186124257 :question/worked-solution "First,  notice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $A=PDP^{-1},$ we can compute $A^k$ by using the formula \n\n$$\nA^{k} = PD^{k}P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{100} & =P{D}^{100}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{100}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{100} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {0}^{100}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124258 :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $E,$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186124258 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122795 17592186122796 17592186122797 17592186122798 17592186122799 17592186122801 17592186122802 17592186122803 17592186122804 17592186122805 17592186122867 17592186122868 17592186122869 17592186122870 17592186122871 17592186123029 17592186123030 17592186123031 17592186123032 17592186123033 17592186306812 17592186306813 17592186306814 17592186306815 17592186306816 17592186306819 17592186306820 17592186306821 17592186306822 17592186306823 17592186306833 17592186306834 17592186306835 17592186306836 17592186306837 17592186306840 17592186306841 17592186306842 17592186306843 17592186306844 17592186306847 17592186306848 17592186306849 17592186306850 17592186306851 17592186306854 17592186306855 17592186306856 17592186306857 17592186306858 17592186306889 17592186306890 17592186306891 17592186306892 17592186306893 17592186306896 17592186306897 17592186306898 17592186306899 17592186306900 17592186351619 17592186351620 17592186351621 17592186351622 17592186351623] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122794 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122794 :ans